(function () {
  const R = window.RES;
  R.addLines("s", "NCERT XI Part 2 (old)", [{"sec": "Introduction","ids": [360],"t": "s-block: last electron enters the outermost s orbital; groups 1 and 2.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 40,"o": "The s-block elements of the Periodic Table are those in which the last electron enters the outermost s-orbital. As the s-orbital can accommodate only two electrons, two groups (1 & 2) belong to the s-block of the Periodic Table."},{"sec": "Introduction","ids": [361,362,363],"t": "Group 1 (Li, Na, K, Rb, Cs, Fr) = alkali metals: their hydroxides are strongly alkaline.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 40,"o": "Group 1 of the Periodic Table consists of the elements: lithium, sodium, potassium, rubidium, caesium and francium. They are collectively known as the alkali metals. These are so called because they form hydroxides on reaction with water which are strongly alkaline in nature."},{"sec": "Introduction","ids": [364,365,366],"t": "Group 2 (Be, Mg, Ca, Sr, Ba, Ra) = alkaline earth metals (Be excluded): oxides/hydroxides alkaline, oxides found in the earth's crust.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 40,"o": "The elements of Group 2 include beryllium, magnesium, calcium, strontium, barium and radium. These elements with the exception of beryllium are commonly known as the alkaline earth metals. These are so called because their oxides and hydroxides are alkaline in nature and these metal oxides are found in the earth's crust."},{"sec": "Introduction","ids": [368],"t": "Fr is highly radioactive (^223Fr, t½ 21 min).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 40,"o": "Francium is highly radioactive; its longest-lived isotope ^223Fr has a half-life of only 21 minutes."},{"sec": "Introduction","ids": [373,374,375],"t": "Li and Be are anomalous and resemble the second element of the next group: **Li ~ Mg, Be ~ Al**.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 41,"o": "Lithium and beryllium, the first elements of Group 1 and Group 2 respectively exhibit some properties which are different from those of the other members of the respective group. In these anomalous properties they resemble the second element of the following group. Thus, lithium shows similarities to magnesium and beryllium to aluminium in many of their properties."},{"sec": "Introduction","ids": [376,377],"t": "Diagonal relationship: similar ionic size and/or charge/radius ratio.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 41,"o": "This type of diagonal similarity is commonly referred to as diagonal relationship in the periodic table. The diagonal relationship is due to the similarity in ionic sizes and/or charge/radius ratio of the elements."},{"sec": "Alkali metals: atomic and physical properties","ids": [382,383,384,385],"t": "Alkali metals: ns^1 over a noble-gas core; most electropositive metals; readily form M^+; never found free.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 41,"o": "All the alkali metals have one valence electron, ns^1 outside the noble gas core. The loosely held s-electron in the outermost valence shell of these elements makes them the most electropositive metals. They readily lose electron to give monovalent M^+ ions. Hence they are never found in free state in nature."},{"sec": "Alkali metals: atomic and physical properties","ids": [392,393,394],"t": "Largest atoms in their periods; size increases down the group; M^+ smaller than M.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 41,"o": "The alkali metal atoms have the largest sizes in a particular period of the periodic table. With increase in atomic number, the atom becomes larger. The monovalent ions (M^+) are smaller than the parent atom."},{"sec": "Alkali metals: atomic and physical properties","ids": [395],"t": "∆iH low and decreasing Li → Cs: size increase outweighs nuclear charge; outer electron well screened.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 41,"o": "The ionization enthalpies of the alkali metals are considerably low and decrease down the group from Li to Cs. This is because the effect of increasing size outweighs the increasing nuclear charge, and the outermost electron is very well screened from the nuclear charge."},{"sec": "Alkali metals: atomic and physical properties","ids": [396,397],"t": "Hydration enthalpy: Li^+ > Na^+ > K^+ > Rb^+ > Cs^+ (decreases with ionic size).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 41,"o": "The hydration enthalpies of alkali metal ions decrease with increase in ionic sizes. Li^+ > Na^+ > K^+ > Rb^+ > Cs^+"},{"sec": "Alkali metals: atomic and physical properties","ids": [397],"t": "**Li^+ is most hydrated, so Li salts are mostly hydrated** (e.g. LiCl·2H2O).","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 41,"o": "Li^+ has maximum degree of hydration and for this reason lithium salts are mostly hydrated, e.g., LiCl.2H2O"},{"sec": "Alkali metals: atomic and physical properties","ids": [397],"t": "Silvery white, soft, light.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 41,"o": "All the alkali metals are silvery white, soft and light metals."},{"sec": "Alkali metals: atomic and physical properties","ids": [398],"t": "Low density, increasing Li → Cs, **but K is lighter than Na**.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 41,"o": "Because of the large size, these elements have low density which increases down the group from Li to Cs. However, potassium is lighter than sodium."},{"sec": "Alkali metals: atomic and physical properties","ids": [399],"t": "Low m.p./b.p.: weak metallic bonding (one valence electron).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 41,"o": "The melting and boiling points of the alkali metals are low indicating weak metallic bonding due to the presence of only a single valence electron in them."},{"sec": "Alkali metals: atomic and physical properties","ids": [400,401],"t": "Flame colours: flame heat excites the outer electron to a higher level.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 41,"o": "The alkali metals and their salts impart characteristic colour to an oxidizing flame. This is because the heat from the flame excites the outermost orbital electron to a higher energy level."},{"sec": "Alkali metals: atomic and physical properties","ids": [402],"t": "Flame colours: Li crimson red (670.8 nm), Na yellow (589.2 nm), K violet (766.5 nm), Rb red violet (780.0 nm), Cs blue (455.5 nm).","exc": false,"tab": true,"src": "old11","page": 41,"o": "Flame colours: Li crimson red (670.8 nm), Na yellow (589.2 nm), K violet (766.5 nm), Rb red violet (780.0 nm), Cs blue (455.5 nm)."},{"sec": "Alkali metals: atomic and physical properties","ids": [402],"t": "Detected by flame test; estimated by flame photometry or atomic absorption spectroscopy.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 41,"o": "Alkali metals can therefore, be detected by the respective flame tests and can be determined by flame photometry or atomic absorption spectroscopy."},{"sec": "Alkali metals: atomic and physical properties","ids": [403,404],"t": "Light can ionise them, so **Cs and K are used as photoelectric cell electrodes**.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 41,"o": "These elements when irradiated with light, the light energy absorbed may be sufficient to make an atom lose electron. This property makes caesium and potassium useful as electrodes in photoelectric cells."},{"sec": "Alkali metals: atomic and physical properties","ids": [404],"t": "Table 10.1: Ionization enthalpy/kJ mol^-1 Li 520, Na 496, K 419, Rb 403, Cs 376. Hydration enthalpy/kJ mol^-1 -506, -406, -330, -310, -276. Density/g cm^-3 0.53, 0.97, 0.86, 1.53, 1.90. E°(M^+/M)/V -3.04, -2.714, -2.925, -2.930, -2.927. Ionic radius M^+/pm 76, 102, 138, 152, 167.","exc": false,"tab": true,"src": "old11","page": 42,"o": "Table 10.1: Ionization enthalpy/kJ mol^-1 Li 520, Na 496, K 419, Rb 403, Cs 376. Hydration enthalpy/kJ mol^-1 -506, -406, -330, -310, -276. Density/g cm^-3 0.53, 0.97, 0.86, 1.53, 1.90. E°(M^+/M)/V -3.04, -2.714, -2.925, -2.930, -2.927. Ionic radius M^+/pm 76, 102, 138, 152, 167."},{"sec": "Alkali metals: chemical properties","ids": [404,405,413],"t": "4Li + O2 → 2Li2O (oxide); 2Na + O2 → Na2O2 (peroxide); M + O2 → MO2 (superoxide) (M = K, Rb, Cs)","exc": false,"tab": true,"src": "old11","page": 42,"o": "4Li + O2 → 2Li2O (oxide); 2Na + O2 → Na2O2 (peroxide); M + O2 → MO2 (superoxide) (M = K, Rb, Cs)"},{"sec": "Alkali metals: chemical properties","ids": [405],"t": "Alkali metal is +1 in all these oxides.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 42,"o": "In all these oxides the oxidation state of the alkali metal is +1."},{"sec": "Alkali metals: chemical properties","ids": [406],"t": "**Li alone reacts directly with N2 of air → Li3N.**","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 42,"o": "Lithium shows exceptional behaviour in reacting directly with nitrogen of air to form the nitride, Li3N as well."},{"sec": "Alkali metals: chemical properties","ids": [407],"t": "Kept in kerosene (reactive toward air and water).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 42,"o": "Because of their high reactivity towards air and water, they are normally kept in kerosene oil."},{"sec": "Alkali metals: chemical properties","ids": [408,409],"t": "Highly reactive (large size, low ∆iH); reactivity increases down the group.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 42,"o": "The alkali metals are highly reactive due to their large size and low ionization enthalpy. The reactivity of these metals increases down the group."},{"sec": "Alkali metals: chemical properties","ids": [410,411,412],"t": "Tarnish in dry air (oxide, then hydroxide with moisture); in O2: **Li → monoxide, Na → peroxide, others → superoxide**.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 42,"o": "The alkali metals tarnish in dry air due to the formation of their oxides which in turn react with moisture to form hydroxides. They burn vigorously in oxygen forming oxides. Lithium forms monoxide, sodium forms peroxide, the other metals form superoxides."},{"sec": "Alkali metals: chemical properties","ids": [413],"t": "O2^- (superoxide) is stable only with large cations: K, Rb, Cs.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 42,"o": "The superoxide O2^- ion is stable only in the presence of large cations such as K, Rb, Cs."},{"sec": "Alkali metals: chemical properties","ids": [414],"t": "2M + 2H2O → 2M^+ + 2OH^- + H2","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 43,"o": "The alkali metals react with water to form hydroxide and dihydrogen. 2M + 2H2O → 2M^+ + 2OH^- + H2 (M = an alkali metal)"},{"sec": "Alkali metals: chemical properties","ids": [414,415],"t": "**Li has the most negative E° yet reacts with water less vigorously than Na** (Na has the least negative E°): small size, very high hydration energy.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 43,"o": "It may be noted that although lithium has most negative E° value, its reaction with water is less vigorous than that of sodium which has the least negative E° value among the alkali metals. This behaviour of lithium is attributed to its small size and very high hydration energy."},{"sec": "Alkali metals: chemical properties","ids": [416,417],"t": "Others react explosively with water; also react with proton donors (alcohol, gaseous NH3, alkynes).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 43,"o": "Other metals of the group react explosively with water. They also react with proton donors such as alcohol, gaseous ammonia and alkynes."},{"sec": "Alkali metals: chemical properties","ids": [418,419],"t": "With H2 at ~673 K (Li at 1073 K): ionic hydrides with high m.p. 2M + H2 → 2M^+H^-","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 43,"o": "The alkali metals react with dihydrogen at about 673K (lithium at 1073K) to form hydrides. All the alkali metal hydrides are ionic solids with high melting points. 2M + H2 → 2M^+H^-"},{"sec": "Alkali metals: chemical properties","ids": [419,420,421],"t": "With halogens: ionic M^+X^-, **but Li halides are somewhat covalent** (high polarising power of Li^+).","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 43,"o": "The alkali metals readily react vigorously with halogens to form ionic halides, M^+X^-. However, lithium halides are somewhat covalent. It is because of the high polarisation capability of lithium ion."},{"sec": "Alkali metals: chemical properties","ids": [422,423],"t": "Small Li^+ distorts large anions most, so **LiI is the most covalent** Li halide.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 43,"o": "The Li^+ ion is very small in size and has high tendency to distort electron cloud around the negative halide ion. Since anion with large size can be easily distorted, among halides, lithium iodide is the most covalent in nature."},{"sec": "Alkali metals: chemical properties","ids": [424],"t": "Strong reducing agents: **Li strongest, Na weakest**.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 43,"o": "The alkali metals are strong reducing agents, lithium being the most and sodium the least powerful."},{"sec": "Alkali metals: chemical properties","ids": [425],"t": "E° (reducing power) covers: M(s) → M(g) (sublimation); M(g) → M^+(g) + e^- (ionization); M^+(g) + H2O → M^+(aq) (hydration).","exc": false,"tab": true,"src": "old11","page": 43,"o": "The standard electrode potential (E°) which measures the reducing power represents the overall change: M(s) → M(g) (sublimation enthalpy); M(g) → M^+(g) + e^- (ionization enthalpy); M^+(g) + H2O → M^+(aq) (hydration enthalpy)."},{"sec": "Alkali metals: chemical properties","ids": [425],"t": "Li^+ has the highest hydration enthalpy, which gives Li its most negative E° and highest reducing power.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 43,"o": "With the small size of its ion, lithium has the highest hydration enthalpy which accounts for its high negative E° value and its high reducing power."},{"sec": "Alkali metals: chemical properties","ids": [427],"t": "Dissolve in liquid NH3: deep blue, conducting solutions.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 43,"o": "The alkali metals dissolve in liquid ammonia giving deep blue solutions which are conducting in nature."},{"sec": "Alkali metals: chemical properties","ids": [428],"t": "M + (x + y)NH3 → [M(NH3)x]^+ + [e(NH3)y]^-","exc": false,"tab": true,"src": "old11","page": 43,"o": "M + (x + y)NH3 → [M(NH3)x]^+ + [e(NH3)y]^-"},{"sec": "Alkali metals: chemical properties","ids": [428],"t": "Blue colour from the ammoniated electron absorbing visible light.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 43,"o": "The blue colour of the solution is due to the ammoniated electron which absorbs energy in the visible region of light and thus imparts blue colour to the solution."},{"sec": "Alkali metals: chemical properties","ids": [429],"t": "Solutions are paramagnetic; on standing they slowly liberate H2 and form amide.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 43,"o": "The solutions are paramagnetic and on standing slowly liberate hydrogen resulting in the formation of amide."},{"sec": "Alkali metals: chemical properties","ids": [430],"t": "M^+(am) + e^- + NH3(l) → MNH2(am) + ½H2(g) (where 'am' denotes solution in ammonia.)","exc": false,"tab": true,"src": "old11","page": 43,"o": "M^+(am) + e^- + NH3(l) → MNH2(am) + ½H2(g) (where 'am' denotes solution in ammonia.)"},{"sec": "Alkali metals: chemical properties","ids": [430],"t": "**Concentrated solution: blue → bronze, and diamagnetic.**","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 43,"o": "In concentrated solution, the blue colour changes to bronze colour and becomes diamagnetic."},{"sec": "Alkali metals: uses","ids": [431],"t": "Li alloys: with Pb ('white metal' bearings), with Al (aircraft parts), with Mg (armour plates).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 43,"o": "Lithium metal is used to make useful alloys, for example with lead to make 'white metal' bearings for motor engines, with aluminium to make aircraft parts, and with magnesium to make armour plates."},{"sec": "Alkali metals: uses","ids": [432,433],"t": "Li: thermonuclear reactions; electrochemical cells.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 43,"o": "It is used in thermonuclear reactions. Lithium is also used to make electrochemical cells."},{"sec": "Alkali metals: uses","ids": [434,435],"t": "Na/Pb alloy used to make PbEt4 and PbMe4 (old anti-knock additives).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 43,"o": "Sodium is used to make a Na/Pb alloy needed to make PbEt4 and PbMe4. These organolead compounds were earlier used as anti-knock additives to petrol, but nowadays vehicles use lead-free petrol."},{"sec": "Alkali metals: uses","ids": [436],"t": "Liquid Na: coolant in fast breeder reactors.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 43,"o": "Liquid sodium metal is used as a coolant in fast breeder nuclear reactors."},{"sec": "Alkali metals: uses","ids": [439,440,441],"t": "KCl: fertiliser. KOH: soft soap; excellent CO2 absorbent.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 44,"o": "Potassium chloride is used as a fertilizer. Potassium hydroxide is used in the manufacture of soft soap. It is also used as an excellent absorbent of carbon dioxide."},{"sec": "Alkali metals: uses","ids": [442],"t": "Cs: photoelectric cells.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 44,"o": "Caesium is used in devising photoelectric cells."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkali metals","ids": [442],"t": "Alkali metal compounds are generally ionic.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 44,"o": "All the common compounds of the alkali metals are generally ionic in nature."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkali metals","ids": [443],"t": "In excess air: **Li → mainly Li2O (some Li2O2); Na → Na2O2 (some NaO2); K, Rb, Cs → MO2**.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 44,"o": "On combustion in excess of air, lithium forms mainly the oxide, Li2O (plus some peroxide Li2O2), sodium forms the peroxide, Na2O2 (and some superoxide NaO2) whilst potassium, rubidium and caesium form the superoxides, MO2."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkali metals","ids": [444],"t": "Pure M2O, M2O2, MO2 can be made under suitable conditions.","exc": false,"tab": true,"src": "old11","page": 44,"o": "Under appropriate conditions pure compounds M2O, M2O2 and MO2 may be prepared."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkali metals","ids": [445],"t": "Peroxide/superoxide stability rises with cation size: large anions stabilised by large cations (lattice energy).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 44,"o": "The increasing stability of the peroxide or superoxide, as the size of the metal ion increases, is due to the stabilisation of large anions by larger cations through lattice energy effects."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkali metals","ids": [446],"t": "These oxides are easily hydrolysed by water to form the hydroxides: M2O + H2O → 2M^+ + 2OH^-; M2O2 + 2H2O → 2M^+ + 2OH^- + H2O2; 2MO2 + 2H2O → 2M^+ + 2OH^- + H2O2 + O2","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 44,"o": "These oxides are easily hydrolysed by water to form the hydroxides: M2O + H2O → 2M^+ + 2OH^-; M2O2 + 2H2O → 2M^+ + 2OH^- + H2O2; 2MO2 + 2H2O → 2M^+ + 2OH^- + H2O2 + O2"},{"sec": "Compounds of alkali metals","ids": [446,447],"t": "**Oxides and peroxides colourless when pure; superoxides yellow or orange and paramagnetic.**","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 44,"o": "The oxides and the peroxides are colourless when pure, but the superoxides are yellow or orange in colour. The superoxides are also paramagnetic."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkali metals","ids": [448],"t": "Na2O2: widely used oxidising agent.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 44,"o": "Sodium peroxide is widely used as an oxidising agent in inorganic chemistry."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkali metals","ids": [450],"t": "O2^- is paramagnetic: one unpaired electron in π*2p.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 44,"o": "The superoxide O2^- is paramagnetic because of one unpaired electron in π*2p molecular orbital."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkali metals","ids": [450,451],"t": "Hydroxides: white crystalline; the strongest bases; dissolve with much heat (intense hydration).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 44,"o": "The hydroxides which are obtained by the reaction of the oxides with water are all white crystalline solids. The alkali metal hydroxides are the strongest of all bases and dissolve freely in water with evolution of much heat on account of intense hydration."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkali metals","ids": [451],"t": "Halides MX: high-melting, colourless crystalline solids.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 44,"o": "The alkali metal halides, MX, (X = F, Cl, Br, I) are all high melting, colourless crystalline solids."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkali metals","ids": [452],"t": "Made from the oxide, hydroxide or carbonate + aqueous HX.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 44,"o": "They can be prepared by the reaction of the appropriate oxide, hydroxide or carbonate with aqueous hydrohalic acid (HX)."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkali metals","ids": [453],"t": "∆fH° of **fluorides becomes less negative down the group**; for chlorides, bromides, iodides it becomes more negative.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 44,"o": "All of these halides have high negative enthalpies of formation; the ∆fH° values for fluorides become less negative as we go down the group, whilst the reverse is true for ∆fH° for chlorides, bromides and iodides."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkali metals","ids": [454,455],"t": "For a given metal ∆fH° becomes less negative F → I; m.p./b.p.: fluoride > chloride > bromide > iodide.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 44,"o": "For a given metal ∆fH° always becomes less negative from fluoride to iodide. The melting and boiling points always follow the trend: fluoride > chloride > bromide > iodide."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkali metals","ids": [456],"t": "All alkali halides are water-soluble.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 44,"o": "All these halides are soluble in water."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkali metals","ids": [457],"t": "**LiF poorly soluble: high lattice enthalpy. CsI poorly soluble: low hydration enthalpy of both ions.**","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 44,"o": "The low solubility of LiF in water is due to its high lattice enthalpy whereas the low solubility of CsI is due to smaller hydration enthalpy of its two ions."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkali metals","ids": [458],"t": "Other Li halides dissolve in ethanol, acetone, ethyl acetate; LiCl also in pyridine.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 44,"o": "Other halides of lithium are soluble in ethanol, acetone and ethylacetate; LiCl is soluble in pyridine also."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkali metals","ids": [459,460],"t": "Form salts with all oxo-acids; generally water-soluble and thermally stable.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 44,"o": "The alkali metals form salts with all the oxo-acids. They are generally soluble in water and thermally stable."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkali metals","ids": [461,462],"t": "M2CO3 and most MHCO3 are highly heat-stable; stability increases down the group.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 44,"o": "Their carbonates (M2CO3) and in most cases the hydrogencarbonates (MHCO3) also are highly stable to heat. As the electropositive character increases down the group, the stability of the carbonates and hydrogencarbonates increases."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkali metals","ids": [463,464],"t": "**Li2CO3 is not very stable**: small Li^+ polarises CO3^2-, giving Li2O + CO2. **LiHCO3 doesn't exist as a solid.**","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 44,"o": "Lithium carbonate is not so stable to heat; lithium being very small in size polarises a large CO3^2- ion leading to the formation of more stable Li2O and CO2. Its hydrogencarbonate does not exist as a solid."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkali metals","ids": [727,728],"t": "Oxides and hydroxides of alkali metals are water-soluble strong alkalies.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 54,"o": "The compounds of alkali metals are predominantly ionic. Their oxides and hydroxides are soluble in water forming strong alkalies."},{"sec": "Anomalous properties of lithium","ids": [465,466],"t": "Li anomaly: very small atom/ion and high polarising power (charge/radius), so covalent character and solubility in organic solvents.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 45,"o": "The anomalous behaviour of lithium is due to the: (i) exceptionally small size of its atom and ion, and (ii) high polarising power (i.e., charge/radius ratio). As a result, there is increased covalent character of lithium compounds which is responsible for their solubility in organic solvents."},{"sec": "Anomalous properties of lithium","ids": [467,468],"t": "Li is much harder, with higher m.p./b.p. than other alkali metals.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 45,"o": "Lithium is much harder. Its m.p. and b.p. are higher than the other alkali metals."},{"sec": "Anomalous properties of lithium","ids": [469,470],"t": "Li: least reactive yet strongest reducing agent; burns in air to Li2O and Li3N.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 45,"o": "Lithium is least reactive but the strongest reducing agent among all the alkali metals. On combustion in air it forms mainly monoxide, Li2O and the nitride, Li3N unlike other alkali metals."},{"sec": "Anomalous properties of lithium","ids": [471],"t": "LiCl is deliquescent and forms LiCl·2H2O; other alkali chlorides form no hydrates.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 45,"o": "LiCl is deliquescent and crystallises as a hydrate, LiCl.2H2O whereas other alkali metal chlorides do not form hydrates."},{"sec": "Anomalous properties of lithium","ids": [472],"t": "LiHCO3 is not obtained as a solid; others are.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 45,"o": "Lithium hydrogencarbonate is not obtained in the solid form while all other elements form solid hydrogencarbonates."},{"sec": "Anomalous properties of lithium","ids": [473],"t": "Li forms no ethynide with ethyne.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 45,"o": "Lithium unlike other alkali metals forms no ethynide on reaction with ethyne."},{"sec": "Anomalous properties of lithium","ids": [474],"t": "**LiNO3 → Li2O on heating; other MNO3 → MNO2.** 4LiNO3 → 2Li2O + 4NO2 + O2; 2NaNO3 → 2NaNO2 + O2","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 45,"o": "Lithium nitrate when heated gives lithium oxide, Li2O, whereas other alkali metal nitrates decompose to give the corresponding nitrite. 4LiNO3 → 2Li2O + 4NO2 + O2; 2NaNO3 → 2NaNO2 + O2"},{"sec": "Anomalous properties of lithium","ids": [474],"t": "LiF and Li2O are much less soluble than the other alkali metals' compounds.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 45,"o": "LiF and Li2O are comparatively much less soluble in water than the corresponding compounds of other alkali metals."},{"sec": "Anomalous properties of lithium","ids": [474],"t": "Li ~ Mg because of similar size: atomic Li 152, Mg 160 pm; ionic Li^+ 76, Mg^2+ 72 pm.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 45,"o": "The similarity between lithium and magnesium is particularly striking and arises because of their similar sizes: atomic radii, Li = 152 pm, Mg = 160 pm; ionic radii: Li^+ = 76 pm, Mg^2+ = 72 pm."},{"sec": "Anomalous properties of lithium","ids": [475,476],"t": "Li and Mg: harder and lighter than their group mates; react slowly with water.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 45,"o": "Both lithium and magnesium are harder and lighter than other elements in the respective groups. Lithium and magnesium react slowly with water."},{"sec": "Anomalous properties of lithium","ids": [477,478],"t": "Oxides and hydroxides much less soluble; hydroxides decompose on heating; both form nitrides (Li3N, Mg3N2) directly.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 45,"o": "Their oxides and hydroxides are much less soluble and their hydroxides decompose on heating. Both form a nitride, Li3N and Mg3N2, by direct combination with nitrogen."},{"sec": "Anomalous properties of lithium","ids": [479],"t": "Li2O and MgO form no superoxide.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 45,"o": "The oxides, Li2O and MgO do not combine with excess oxygen to give any superoxide."},{"sec": "Anomalous properties of lithium","ids": [480,481],"t": "Carbonates decompose easily to oxide + CO2; no solid hydrogencarbonates.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 45,"o": "The carbonates of lithium and magnesium decompose easily on heating to form the oxides and CO2. Solid hydrogencarbonates are not formed by lithium and magnesium."},{"sec": "Anomalous properties of lithium","ids": [482,483],"t": "LiCl and MgCl2: soluble in ethanol; deliquescent; hydrates LiCl·2H2O, MgCl2·8H2O.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 45,"o": "Both LiCl and MgCl2 are soluble in ethanol. Both LiCl and MgCl2 are deliquescent and crystallise from aqueous solution as hydrates, LiCl·2H2O and MgCl2·8H2O."},{"sec": "Sodium carbonate","ids": [484,485],"t": "**Solvay process** uses the low solubility of NaHCO3, which precipitates from NaCl + NH4HCO3.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 45,"o": "Sodium carbonate is generally prepared by Solvay Process. In this process, advantage is taken of the low solubility of sodium hydrogencarbonate whereby it gets precipitated in the reaction of sodium chloride with ammonium hydrogencarbonate."},{"sec": "Sodium carbonate","ids": [486],"t": "NH4HCO3 forms when CO2 is passed into concentrated brine saturated with NH3 (via (NH4)2CO3).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 45,"o": "The latter is prepared by passing CO2 to a concentrated solution of sodium chloride saturated with ammonia, where ammonium carbonate followed by ammonium hydrogencarbonate are formed."},{"sec": "Sodium carbonate","ids": [487],"t": "2NH3 + H2O + CO2 → (NH4)2CO3; (NH4)2CO3 + H2O + CO2 → 2NH4HCO3; NH4HCO3 + NaCl → NH4Cl + NaHCO3","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 45,"o": "2NH3 + H2O + CO2 → (NH4)2CO3; (NH4)2CO3 + H2O + CO2 → 2NH4HCO3; NH4HCO3 + NaCl → NH4Cl + NaHCO3"},{"sec": "Sodium carbonate","ids": [487,488,489],"t": "NaHCO3 crystals are heated: 2NaHCO3 → Na2CO3 + CO2 + H2O","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 45,"o": "Sodium hydrogencarbonate crystal separates. These are heated to give sodium carbonate. 2NaHCO3 → Na2CO3 + CO2 + H2O"},{"sec": "Sodium carbonate","ids": [489,490],"t": "NH3 recovered with Ca(OH)2 (CaCl2 by-product): 2NH4Cl + Ca(OH)2 → 2NH3 + CaCl2 + H2O","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 46,"o": "In this process NH3 is recovered when the solution containing NH4Cl is treated with Ca(OH)2. Calcium chloride is obtained as a by-product. 2NH4Cl + Ca(OH)2 → 2NH3 + CaCl2 + H2O"},{"sec": "Sodium carbonate","ids": [490],"t": "**Solvay can't make K2CO3: KHCO3 is too soluble to precipitate.**","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 46,"o": "Solvay process cannot be extended to the manufacture of potassium carbonate because potassium hydrogencarbonate is too soluble to be precipitated by the addition of ammonium hydrogencarbonate to a saturated solution of potassium chloride."},{"sec": "Sodium carbonate","ids": [491,492,493],"t": "Na2CO3·10H2O = washing soda; white crystalline, readily soluble.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 46,"o": "Sodium carbonate is a white crystalline solid which exists as a decahydrate, Na2CO3·10H2O. This is also called washing soda. It is readily soluble in water."},{"sec": "Sodium carbonate","ids": [494,495],"t": "Heating: decahydrate → monohydrate; above 373 K anhydrous (white powder, soda ash).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 46,"o": "On heating, the decahydrate loses its water of crystallisation to form monohydrate. Above 373K, the monohydrate becomes completely anhydrous and changes to a white powder called soda ash."},{"sec": "Sodium carbonate","ids": [496],"t": "Na2CO3·10H2O → Na2CO3·H2O + 9H2O (375 K); Na2CO3·H2O → Na2CO3 + H2O (>373 K)","exc": false,"tab": true,"src": "old11","page": 46,"o": "Na2CO3·10H2O → Na2CO3·H2O + 9H2O (375 K); Na2CO3·H2O → Na2CO3 + H2O (>373 K)"},{"sec": "Sodium carbonate","ids": [496,497],"t": "Aqueous Na2CO3 is alkaline (hydrolysis): CO3^2- + H2O → HCO3^- + OH^-","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 46,"o": "Carbonate part of sodium carbonate gets hydrolysed by water to form an alkaline solution. CO3^2- + H2O → HCO3^- + OH^-"},{"sec": "Sodium carbonate","ids": [497,498],"t": "Uses: water softening, laundering, cleaning; glass, soap, borax, caustic soda.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 46,"o": "It is used in water softening, laundering and cleaning. It is used in the manufacture of glass, soap, borax and caustic soda."},{"sec": "Sodium chloride","ids": [504,505],"t": "Crude NaCl contains Na2SO4, CaSO4, CaCl2, MgCl2; CaCl2 and MgCl2 are deliquescent.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 46,"o": "Crude sodium chloride, generally obtained by crystallisation of brine solution, contains sodium sulphate, calcium sulphate, calcium chloride and magnesium chloride as impurities. Calcium chloride, CaCl2, and magnesium chloride, MgCl2 are impurities because they are deliquescent (absorb moisture easily from the atmosphere)."},{"sec": "Sodium chloride","ids": [506,507,508,509],"t": "Purification: dissolve, filter, saturate with HCl gas; pure NaCl crystallises; CaCl2, MgCl2 (more soluble) stay in solution.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 46,"o": "To obtain pure sodium chloride, the crude salt is dissolved in minimum amount of water and filtered to remove insoluble impurities. The solution is then saturated with hydrogen chloride gas. Crystals of pure sodium chloride separate out. Calcium and magnesium chloride, being more soluble than sodium chloride, remain in solution."},{"sec": "Sodium chloride","ids": [510],"t": "NaCl m.p. 1081 K; solubility 36.0 g/100 g water (273 K), barely changes with temperature.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 46,"o": "Sodium chloride melts at 1081K. It has a solubility of 36.0 g in 100 g of water at 273 K. The solubility does not increase appreciably with increase in temperature."},{"sec": "Sodium chloride","ids": [512],"t": "NaCl is used to make Na2O2, NaOH, Na2CO3.","exc": false,"tab": true,"src": "old11","page": 46,"o": "Sodium chloride is used for the preparation of Na2O2, NaOH and Na2CO3."},{"sec": "Sodium hydroxide","ids": [513,514],"t": "**Castner-Kellner cell**: brine electrolysed with Hg cathode, carbon anode.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 46,"o": "Sodium hydroxide is generally prepared commercially by the electrolysis of sodium chloride in Castner-Kellner cell. A brine solution is electrolysed using a mercury cathode and a carbon anode."},{"sec": "Sodium hydroxide","ids": [515,516],"t": "Na forms sodium amalgam at the cathode; Cl2 at the anode.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 46,"o": "Sodium metal discharged at the cathode combines with mercury to form sodium amalgam. Chlorine gas is evolved at the anode."},{"sec": "Sodium hydroxide","ids": [517],"t": "Cathode: Na^+ + e^- → Na-amalgam (Hg); Anode: Cl^- → ½Cl2 + e^-. 2Na-amalgam + 2H2O → 2NaOH + 2Hg + H2","exc": false,"tab": true,"src": "old11","page": 46,"o": "Cathode: Na^+ + e^- → Na-amalgam (Hg); Anode: Cl^- → ½Cl2 + e^-. 2Na-amalgam + 2H2O → 2NaOH + 2Hg + H2"},{"sec": "Sodium hydroxide","ids": [517,518,519],"t": "NaOH: white, translucent, m.p. 591 K; very soluble (strongly alkaline); deliquescent.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 46,"o": "Sodium hydroxide is a white, translucent solid. It melts at 591 K. It is readily soluble in water to give a strong alkaline solution. Crystals of sodium hydroxide are deliquescent."},{"sec": "Sodium hydroxide","ids": [520],"t": "NaOH solution absorbs atmospheric CO2, forming Na2CO3.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 46,"o": "The sodium hydroxide solution at the surface reacts with the CO2 in the atmosphere to form Na2CO3."},{"sec": "Sodium hydroxide","ids": [521],"t": "NaOH uses: soap, paper, artificial silk; petroleum refining; purification of bauxite; mercerising cotton; purifying fats and oils.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 47,"o": "It is used in (i) the manufacture of soap, paper, artificial silk and a number of chemicals, (ii) in petroleum refining, (iii) in the purification of bauxite, (iv) in the textile industries for mercerising cotton fabrics, (v) for the preparation of pure fats and oils, and (vi) as a laboratory reagent."},{"sec": "Sodium hydroxide","ids": [730],"t": "NaOH by Castner-Kellner; Na2CO3 by Solvay.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 54,"o": "Sodium hydroxide is manufactured by Castner-Kellner process and sodium carbonate by Solvay process."},{"sec": "Sodium hydrogencarbonate","ids": [522],"t": "NaHCO3 = baking soda: decomposes on heating, releasing CO2.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 47,"o": "Sodium hydrogencarbonate is known as baking soda because it decomposes on heating to generate bubbles of carbon dioxide (leaving holes in cakes or pastries and making them light and fluffy)."},{"sec": "Sodium hydrogencarbonate","ids": [523,524],"t": "Made by saturating Na2CO3 solution with CO2; less soluble NaHCO3 separates.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 47,"o": "Sodium hydrogencarbonate is made by saturating a solution of sodium carbonate with carbon dioxide. The white crystalline powder of sodium hydrogencarbonate, being less soluble, gets separated out."},{"sec": "Sodium hydrogencarbonate","ids": [525,526],"t": "Na2CO3 + H2O + CO2 → 2NaHCO3. Mild antiseptic; used in fire extinguishers.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 47,"o": "Na2CO3 + H2O + CO2 → 2NaHCO3. Sodium hydrogencarbonate is a mild antiseptic for skin infections. It is used in fire extinguishers."},{"sec": "Alkaline earth metals: atomic and physical properties","ids": [537],"t": "Be differs from the rest and shows diagonal relationship with Al.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 47,"o": "The first element beryllium differs from the rest of the members and shows diagonal relationship to aluminium."},{"sec": "Alkaline earth metals: atomic and physical properties","ids": [539,540],"t": "[noble gas] ns^2; compounds predominantly ionic.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 47,"o": "Their general electronic configuration may be represented as [noble gas] ns^2. Like alkali metals, the compounds of these elements are also predominantly ionic."},{"sec": "Alkaline earth metals: atomic and physical properties","ids": [545,554,555],"t": "Radii smaller than the corresponding alkali metals (higher nuclear charge); increase down the group.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 47,"o": "The atomic and ionic radii of the alkaline earth metals are smaller than those of the corresponding alkali metals in the same periods. This is due to the increased nuclear charge in these elements. Within the group, the atomic and ionic radii increase with increase in atomic number."},{"sec": "Alkaline earth metals: atomic and physical properties","ids": [551,552],"t": "Ionization enthalpy (I)/kJ mol^-1: Be 899, Mg 737, Ca 590, Sr 549, Ba 503, Ra 509. Ionization enthalpy (II)/kJ mol^-1: Be 1757, Mg 1450, Ca 1145, Sr 1064, Ba 965, Ra 979.","exc": false,"tab": true,"src": "old11","page": 48,"o": "Ionization enthalpy (I)/kJ mol^-1: Be 899, Mg 737, Ca 590, Sr 549, Ba 503, Ra 509. Ionization enthalpy (II)/kJ mol^-1: Be 1757, Mg 1450, Ca 1145, Sr 1064, Ba 965, Ra 979."},{"sec": "Alkaline earth metals: atomic and physical properties","ids": [553],"t": "Table 10.2: Ionic radius M^2+/pm Be 31, Mg 72, Ca 100, Sr 118, Ba 135, Ra 148. Hydration enthalpy/kJ mol^-1 -2494, -1921, -1577, -1443, -1305. E°(M^2+/M)/V -1.97, -2.36, -2.84, -2.89, -2.92, -2.92. Density/g cm^-3 1.84, 1.74, 1.55, 2.63, 3.59.","exc": false,"tab": true,"src": "old11","page": 48,"o": "Table 10.2: Ionic radius M^2+/pm Be 31, Mg 72, Ca 100, Sr 118, Ba 135, Ra 148. Hydration enthalpy/kJ mol^-1 -2494, -1921, -1577, -1443, -1305. E°(M^2+/M)/V -1.97, -2.36, -2.84, -2.89, -2.92, -2.92. Density/g cm^-3 1.84, 1.74, 1.55, 2.63, 3.59."},{"sec": "Alkaline earth metals: atomic and physical properties","ids": [556,557],"t": "∆iH low (fairly large atoms) and decreasing down the group.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 48,"o": "The alkaline earth metals have low ionization enthalpies due to fairly large size of the atoms. Since the atomic size increases down the group, their ionization enthalpy decreases."},{"sec": "Alkaline earth metals: atomic and physical properties","ids": [558,559],"t": "∆iH1 higher than group 1 (smaller size).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 48,"o": "The first ionisation enthalpies of the alkaline earth metals are higher than those of the corresponding Group 1 metals. This is due to their small size as compared to the corresponding alkali metals."},{"sec": "Alkaline earth metals: atomic and physical properties","ids": [560],"t": "**∆iH2 of group 2 is smaller than ∆iH2 of the corresponding alkali metals.**","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 48,"o": "It is interesting to note that the second ionisation enthalpies of the alkaline earth metals are smaller than those of the corresponding alkali metals."},{"sec": "Alkaline earth metals: atomic and physical properties","ids": [561],"t": "Hydration enthalpy: Be^2+ > Mg^2+ > Ca^2+ > Sr^2+ > Ba^2+","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 48,"o": "Like alkali metal ions, the hydration enthalpies of alkaline earth metal ions decrease with increase in ionic size down the group. Be^2+ > Mg^2+ > Ca^2+ > Sr^2+ > Ba^2+"},{"sec": "Alkaline earth metals: atomic and physical properties","ids": [561,562],"t": "Hydration enthalpies larger than for alkali ions, so more hydrated compounds: MgCl2·6H2O, CaCl2·6H2O (NaCl, KCl form none).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 48,"o": "The hydration enthalpies of alkaline earth metal ions are larger than those of alkali metal ions. Thus, compounds of alkaline earth metals are more extensively hydrated than those of alkali metals, e.g., MgCl2 and CaCl2 exist as MgCl2.6H2O and CaCl2.6H2O while NaCl and KCl do not form such hydrates."},{"sec": "Alkaline earth metals: atomic and physical properties","ids": [563,564],"t": "Silvery white, lustrous, fairly soft but harder than alkali metals; Be and Mg greyish.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 48,"o": "The alkaline earth metals, in general, are silvery white, lustrous and relatively soft but harder than the alkali metals. Beryllium and magnesium appear to be somewhat greyish."},{"sec": "Alkaline earth metals: atomic and physical properties","ids": [565,566],"t": "m.p./b.p. higher than alkali metals (smaller size), **but the trend is not systematic**.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 48,"o": "The melting and boiling points of these metals are higher than the corresponding alkali metals due to smaller sizes. The trend is, however, not systematic."},{"sec": "Alkaline earth metals: atomic and physical properties","ids": [567],"t": "Strongly electropositive (low ∆iH).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 48,"o": "Because of the low ionisation enthalpies, they are strongly electropositive in nature."},{"sec": "Alkaline earth metals: atomic and physical properties","ids": [568,569],"t": "Flame: **Ca brick red, Sr crimson, Ba apple green**.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 48,"o": "Calcium, strontium and barium impart characteristic brick red, crimson and apple green colours respectively to the flame."},{"sec": "Alkaline earth metals: atomic and physical properties","ids": [571,572],"t": "**Be and Mg give no flame colour**: electrons too strongly bound to be excited.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 49,"o": "The electrons in beryllium and magnesium are too strongly bound to get excited by flame. Hence, these elements do not impart any colour to the flame."},{"sec": "Alkaline earth metals: atomic and physical properties","ids": [573],"t": "Flame test detects Ca, Sr, Ba; estimated by flame photometry.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 49,"o": "The flame test for Ca, Sr and Ba is helpful in their detection in qualitative analysis and estimation by flame photometry."},{"sec": "Alkaline earth metals: atomic and physical properties","ids": [574],"t": "High electrical and thermal conductivity.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 49,"o": "The alkaline earth metals like those of alkali metals have high electrical and thermal conductivities which are typical characteristics of metals."},{"sec": "Alkaline earth metals: chemical properties","ids": [575,576],"t": "Less reactive than alkali metals; reactivity increases down the group.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 49,"o": "The alkaline earth metals are less reactive than the alkali metals. The reactivity of these elements increases on going down the group."},{"sec": "Alkaline earth metals: chemical properties","ids": [577],"t": "**Be and Mg are kinetically inert to O2 and water** (oxide film).","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 49,"o": "Beryllium and magnesium are kinetically inert to oxygen and water because of the formation of an oxide film on their surface."},{"sec": "Alkaline earth metals: chemical properties","ids": [578,579],"t": "Powdered Be burns to BeO + Be3N2; Mg burns with dazzling light to MgO + Mg3N2.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 49,"o": "However, powdered beryllium burns brilliantly on ignition in air to give BeO and Be3N2. Magnesium is more electropositive and burns with dazzling brilliance in air to give MgO and Mg3N2."},{"sec": "Alkaline earth metals: chemical properties","ids": [580,581],"t": "Ca, Sr, Ba: readily attacked by air (oxide + nitride); react with cold water with increasing vigour.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 49,"o": "Calcium, strontium and barium are readily attacked by air to form the oxide and nitride. They also react with water with increasing vigour even in cold to form hydroxides."},{"sec": "Alkaline earth metals: chemical properties","ids": [582,583],"t": "M + X2 → MX2 at elevated temperature.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 49,"o": "All the alkaline earth metals combine with halogen at elevated temperatures forming their halides. M + X2 → MX2 (X = F, Cl, Br, I)"},{"sec": "Alkaline earth metals: chemical properties","ids": [583],"t": "BeF2 best made by thermal decomposition of (NH4)2BeF4; BeCl2 from the oxide.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 49,"o": "Thermal decomposition of (NH4)2BeF4 is the best route for the preparation of BeF2, and BeCl2 is conveniently made from the oxide."},{"sec": "Alkaline earth metals: chemical properties","ids": [584],"t": "BeO + C + Cl2 → BeCl2 + CO (600-800K)","exc": false,"tab": true,"src": "old11","page": 49,"o": "BeO + C + Cl2 → BeCl2 + CO (600-800K)"},{"sec": "Alkaline earth metals: chemical properties","ids": [584],"t": "**All except Be form MH2 directly on heating**; BeH2 via 2BeCl2 + LiAlH4 → 2BeH2 + LiCl + AlCl3","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 49,"o": "All the elements except beryllium combine with hydrogen upon heating to form their hydrides, MH2. BeH2, however, can be prepared by the reaction of BeCl2 with LiAlH4. 2BeCl2 + LiAlH4 → 2BeH2 + LiCl + AlCl3"},{"sec": "Alkaline earth metals: chemical properties","ids": [584,585],"t": "M + 2HCl → MCl2 + H2","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 49,"o": "The alkaline earth metals readily react with acids liberating dihydrogen. M + 2HCl → MCl2 + H2"},{"sec": "Alkaline earth metals: chemical properties","ids": [585,586,587],"t": "Strong reducing agents (large negative E°), but weaker than alkali metals.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 49,"o": "Like alkali metals, the alkaline earth metals are strong reducing agents. This is indicated by large negative values of their reduction potentials. However their reducing power is less than those of their corresponding alkali metals."},{"sec": "Alkaline earth metals: chemical properties","ids": [588,589],"t": "**Be has the least negative E°**, yet reduces because of the large hydration energy of small Be^2+ and the high atomisation enthalpy of the metal.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 49,"o": "Beryllium has less negative value compared to other alkaline earth metals. However, its reducing nature is due to large hydration energy associated with the small size of Be^2+ ion and relatively large value of the atomization enthalpy of the metal."},{"sec": "Alkaline earth metals: chemical properties","ids": [589,590],"t": "In liquid NH3: deep blue-black solutions. M + (x + y)NH3 → [M(NH3)x]^2+ + 2[e(NH3)y]^-; ammoniates [M(NH3)6]^2+ can be recovered.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 49,"o": "Like alkali metals, the alkaline earth metals dissolve in liquid ammonia to give deep blue black solutions forming ammoniated ions. M + (x + y)NH3 → [M(NH3)x]^2+ + 2[e(NH3)y]^-. From these solutions, the ammoniates, [M(NH3)6]^2+ can be recovered."},{"sec": "Alkaline earth metals: uses","ids": [591,592,593],"t": "Be: alloys (Cu-Be for high-strength springs); metallic Be for X-ray tube windows.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 49,"o": "Beryllium is used in the manufacture of alloys. Copper-beryllium alloys are used in the preparation of high strength springs. Metallic beryllium is used for making windows of X-ray tubes."},{"sec": "Alkaline earth metals: uses","ids": [594,595],"t": "Mg alloys with Al, Zn, Mn, Sn; Mg-Al alloys (light) for aircraft.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 49,"o": "Magnesium forms alloys with aluminium, zinc, manganese and tin. Magnesium-aluminium alloys being light in mass are used in air-craft construction."},{"sec": "Alkaline earth metals: uses","ids": [596],"t": "Mg powder/ribbon: flash powders and bulbs, incendiary bombs, signals.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 49,"o": "Magnesium (powder and ribbon) is used in flash powders and bulbs, incendiary bombs and signals."},{"sec": "Alkaline earth metals: uses","ids": [597,598],"t": "Milk of magnesia (Mg(OH)2 suspension): antacid. MgCO3: toothpaste.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 49,"o": "A suspension of magnesium hydroxide in water (called milk of magnesia) is used as antacid in medicine. Magnesium carbonate is an ingredient of toothpaste."},{"sec": "Alkaline earth metals: uses","ids": [599],"t": "Ca: extracts metals from oxides hard to reduce with carbon.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 49,"o": "Calcium is used in the extraction of metals from oxides which are difficult to reduce with carbon."},{"sec": "Alkaline earth metals: uses","ids": [600],"t": "Ca and Ba remove air from vacuum tubes (react with O2 and N2 at high T).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 49,"o": "Calcium and barium metals, owing to their reactivity with oxygen and nitrogen at elevated temperatures, have often been used to remove air from vacuum tubes."},{"sec": "Alkaline earth metals: uses","ids": [601],"t": "Ra salts: radiotherapy.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 49,"o": "Radium salts are used in radiotherapy, for example, in the treatment of cancer."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkaline earth metals","ids": [602,603,604],"t": "M^2+ predominant; compounds ionic but less so than alkali compounds (higher charge, smaller size).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 50,"o": "The dipositive oxidation state (M^2+) is the predominant valence of Group 2 elements. The alkaline earth metals form compounds which are predominantly ionic but less ionic than the corresponding compounds of alkali metals. This is due to increased nuclear charge and smaller size."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkaline earth metals","ids": [605],"t": "Be and Mg compounds are more covalent than those of Ca, Sr, Ba.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 50,"o": "The oxides and other compounds of beryllium and magnesium are more covalent than those formed by the heavier and large sized members (Ca, Sr, Ba)."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkaline earth metals","ids": [607,608],"t": "Burn to MO with rock-salt structure, **except BeO (essentially covalent)**.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 50,"o": "The alkaline earth metals burn in oxygen to form the monoxide, MO which, except for BeO, have rock-salt structure. The BeO is essentially covalent in nature."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkaline earth metals","ids": [609],"t": "High ∆fH of oxides, so very heat-stable.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 50,"o": "The enthalpies of formation of these oxides are quite high and consequently they are very stable to heat."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkaline earth metals","ids": [610,611],"t": "**BeO is amphoteric**; the other oxides are basic and give sparingly soluble hydroxides with water.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 50,"o": "BeO is amphoteric while oxides of other elements are ionic in nature. All these oxides except BeO are basic in nature and react with water to form sparingly soluble hydroxides."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkaline earth metals","ids": [612],"t": "MO + H2O → M(OH)2. Solubility, thermal stability and basicity increase Mg(OH)2 → Ba(OH)2.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 50,"o": "MO + H2O → M(OH)2. The solubility, thermal stability and the basic character of these hydroxides increase with increasing atomic number from Mg(OH)2 to Ba(OH)2."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkaline earth metals","ids": [613],"t": "Less basic and less stable than alkali hydroxides.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 50,"o": "The alkaline earth metal hydroxides are, however, less basic and less stable than alkali metal hydroxides."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkaline earth metals","ids": [614,615],"t": "**Be(OH)2 is amphoteric**: Be(OH)2 + 2OH^- → [Be(OH)4]^2- (beryllate); Be(OH)2 + 2HCl + 2H2O → [Be(OH)4]Cl2","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 50,"o": "Beryllium hydroxide is amphoteric in nature as it reacts with acid and alkali both. Be(OH)2 + 2OH^- → [Be(OH)4]^2- (beryllate ion); Be(OH)2 + 2HCl + 2H2O → [Be(OH)4]Cl2"},{"sec": "Compounds of alkaline earth metals","ids": [615,616],"t": "All group 2 halides are ionic **except Be halides** (covalent, soluble in organic solvents).","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 50,"o": "Except for beryllium halides, all other halides of alkaline earth metals are ionic in nature. Beryllium halides are essentially covalent and soluble in organic solvents."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkaline earth metals","ids": [617],"t": "BeCl2: chain structure in solid; chloro-bridged dimer in vapour; linear monomer at ~1200 K.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 50,"o": "Beryllium chloride has a chain structure in the solid state. In the vapour phase BeCl2 tends to form a chloro-bridged dimer which dissociates into the linear monomer at high temperatures of the order of 1200 K."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkaline earth metals","ids": [617],"t": "Halide hydrate formation decreases down the group: MgCl2·8H2O, CaCl2·6H2O, SrCl2·6H2O, BaCl2·2H2O.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 50,"o": "The tendency to form halide hydrates gradually decreases (for example, MgCl2·8H2O, CaCl2·6H2O, SrCl2·6H2O and BaCl2·2H2O) down the group."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkaline earth metals","ids": [618],"t": "**Hydrated halides of Ca, Sr, Ba dehydrate on heating; those of Be and Mg hydrolyse.**","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 50,"o": "The dehydration of hydrated chlorides, bromides and iodides of Ca, Sr and Ba can be achieved on heating; however, the corresponding hydrated halides of Be and Mg on heating suffer hydrolysis."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkaline earth metals","ids": [619],"t": "Fluorides less soluble than chlorides (high lattice energy).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 50,"o": "The fluorides are relatively less soluble than the chlorides owing to their high lattice energies."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkaline earth metals","ids": [621],"t": "Carbonates are insoluble; precipitated by Na2CO3 or (NH4)2CO3.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 50,"o": "Carbonates of alkaline earth metals are insoluble in water and can be precipitated by addition of a sodium or ammonium carbonate solution to a solution of a soluble salt of these metals."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkaline earth metals","ids": [622,623],"t": "Carbonate solubility decreases down the group; all decompose on heating to oxide + CO2.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 50,"o": "The solubility of carbonates in water decreases as the atomic number of the metal ion increases. All the carbonates decompose on heating to give carbon dioxide and the oxide."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkaline earth metals","ids": [624,625],"t": "**BeCO3 is unstable** (kept only under CO2); thermal stability rises with cation size.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 50,"o": "Beryllium carbonate is unstable and can be kept only in the atmosphere of CO2. The thermal stability increases with increasing cationic size."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkaline earth metals","ids": [626,627],"t": "Sulphates: white, heat-stable. **BeSO4, MgSO4 soluble**; solubility falls CaSO4 → BaSO4.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 50,"o": "The sulphates of the alkaline earth metals are all white solids and stable to heat. BeSO4 and MgSO4 are readily soluble in water; the solubility decreases from CaSO4 to BaSO4."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkaline earth metals","ids": [628],"t": "BeSO4, MgSO4 soluble because the high hydration enthalpy of Be^2+, Mg^2+ beats the lattice enthalpy.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 50,"o": "The greater hydration enthalpies of Be^2+ and Mg^2+ ions overcome the lattice enthalpy factor and therefore their sulphates are soluble in water."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkaline earth metals","ids": [629,630,631],"t": "Nitrates from carbonates + dilute HNO3; Mg(NO3)2·6H2O but anhydrous Ba(NO3)2 (hydration falls with size).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 50,"o": "The nitrates are made by dissolution of the carbonates in dilute nitric acid. Magnesium nitrate crystallises with six molecules of water, whereas barium nitrate crystallises as the anhydrous salt. This again shows a decreasing tendency to form hydrates with increasing size and decreasing hydration enthalpy."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkaline earth metals","ids": [632],"t": "All nitrates → oxide on heating (like LiNO3): 2M(NO3)2 → 2MO + 4NO2 + O2","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 50,"o": "All of them decompose on heating to give the oxide like lithium nitrate. 2M(NO3)2 → 2MO + 4NO2 + O2 (M = Be, Mg, Ca, Sr, Ba)"},{"sec": "Compounds of alkaline earth metals","ids": [634,635],"t": "**Hydroxide solubility increases down the group**: common anion, so lattice enthalpy falls faster than hydration enthalpy.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 51,"o": "Among alkaline earth metal hydroxides, the anion being common the cationic radius will influence the lattice enthalpy. Since lattice enthalpy decreases much more than the hydration enthalpy with increasing ionic size, the solubility increases as we go down the group."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkaline earth metals","ids": [637,638],"t": "**Carbonate and sulphate solubility decreases down the group**: large anion keeps lattice enthalpy ~constant while hydration enthalpy falls.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 51,"o": "The size of anions being much larger compared to cations, the lattice enthalpy will remain almost constant within a particular group. Since the hydration enthalpies decrease down the group, solubility will decrease as found for alkaline earth metal carbonates and sulphates."},{"sec": "Compounds of alkaline earth metals","ids": [732,733],"t": "Group 2 differs from group 1 (smaller size, higher charge): oxides and hydroxides less basic.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 54,"o": "However, some differences arise because of reduced atomic and ionic sizes and increased cationic charges in case of alkaline earth metals. Their oxides and hydroxides are less basic than the alkali metal oxides and hydroxides."},{"sec": "Anomalous behaviour of beryllium","ids": [641,642],"t": "Be: very small, high ∆iH, so largely covalent compounds that hydrolyse easily.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 51,"o": "Beryllium has exceptionally small atomic and ionic sizes and thus does not compare well with other members of the group. Because of high ionisation enthalpy and small size it forms compounds which are largely covalent and get easily hydrolysed."},{"sec": "Anomalous behaviour of beryllium","ids": [643,644],"t": "**Be max coordination number 4** (only 4 valence orbitals); others reach 6 using d orbitals.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 51,"o": "Beryllium does not exhibit coordination number more than four as in its valence shell there are only four orbitals. The remaining members of the group can have a coordination number of six by making use of d-orbitals."},{"sec": "Anomalous behaviour of beryllium","ids": [645],"t": "Be oxide and hydroxide are amphoteric (others basic).","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 51,"o": "The oxide and hydroxide of beryllium, unlike the hydroxides of other elements in the group, are amphoteric in nature."},{"sec": "Anomalous behaviour of beryllium","ids": [646,647],"t": "Be^2+ radius 31 pm; charge/radius ≈ Al^3+, hence Be ~ Al.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 51,"o": "The ionic radius of Be^2+ is estimated to be 31 pm; the charge/radius ratio is nearly the same as that of the Al^3+ ion. Hence beryllium resembles aluminium in some ways."},{"sec": "Anomalous behaviour of beryllium","ids": [648],"t": "Be, like Al, resists acids (oxide film).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 51,"o": "Like aluminium, beryllium is not readily attacked by acids because of the presence of an oxide film on the surface of the metal."},{"sec": "Anomalous behaviour of beryllium","ids": [648],"t": "Be(OH)2 + excess alkali → [Be(OH)4]^2-, like Al(OH)3 → [Al(OH)4]^-.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 51,"o": "Beryllium hydroxide dissolves in excess of alkali to give a beryllate ion, [Be(OH)4]^2- just as aluminium hydroxide gives aluminate ion, [Al(OH)4]^-."},{"sec": "Anomalous behaviour of beryllium","ids": [649,650,651],"t": "BeCl2 and AlCl3: Cl-bridged in vapour; soluble in organic solvents; strong Lewis acids; Friedel–Crafts catalysts.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 51,"o": "The chlorides of both beryllium and aluminium have Cl^- bridged chloride structure in vapour phase. Both the chlorides are soluble in organic solvents and are strong Lewis acids. They are used as Friedel Craft catalysts."},{"sec": "Anomalous behaviour of beryllium","ids": [652],"t": "Both form complexes: BeF4^2-, AlF6^3-.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 51,"o": "Beryllium and aluminium ions have strong tendency to form complexes, BeF4^2-, AlF6^3-."},{"sec": "Calcium oxide","ids": [656],"t": "CaO: limestone heated in a rotary kiln at 1070–1270 K; CaCO3 → CaO + CO2; CO2 removed continuously to complete the reaction.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 51,"o": "It is prepared on a commercial scale by heating limestone (CaCO3) in a rotary kiln at 1070-1270 K. CaCO3 → CaO + CO2 (heat). The carbon dioxide is removed as soon as it is produced to enable the reaction to proceed to completion."},{"sec": "Calcium oxide","ids": [657,658],"t": "CaO: white amorphous, m.p. 2870 K; absorbs moisture and CO2 from air.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 51,"o": "Calcium oxide is a white amorphous solid. It has a melting point of 2870 K. On exposure to atmosphere, it absorbs moisture and carbon dioxide."},{"sec": "Calcium oxide","ids": [659,660],"t": "CaO + H2O → Ca(OH)2 (slaking); CaO + CO2 → CaCO3","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 52,"o": "CaO + H2O → Ca(OH)2; CaO + CO2 → CaCO3. The addition of limited amount of water breaks the lump of lime. This process is called slaking of lime."},{"sec": "Calcium oxide","ids": [661,662,663],"t": "Quick lime + soda → soda lime. Basic oxide: CaO + SiO2 → CaSiO3; 6CaO + P4O10 → 2Ca3(PO4)2","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 52,"o": "Quick lime slaked with soda gives solid sodalime. Being a basic oxide, it combines with acidic oxides at high temperature. CaO + SiO2 → CaSiO3; 6CaO + P4O10 → 2Ca3(PO4)2"},{"sec": "Calcium oxide","ids": [663,664,665],"t": "CaO uses: cement (cheapest alkali), Na2CO3 from caustic soda, sugar purification, dyestuffs.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 52,"o": "It is an important primary material for manufacturing cement and is the cheapest form of alkali. It is used in the manufacture of sodium carbonate from caustic soda. It is employed in the purification of sugar and in the manufacture of dye stuffs."},{"sec": "Calcium hydroxide","ids": [666,667,668],"t": "Ca(OH)2: CaO + water; white amorphous powder, sparingly soluble; solution = lime water, suspension = milk of lime.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 52,"o": "Calcium hydroxide is prepared by adding water to quick lime, CaO. It is a white amorphous powder. It is sparingly soluble in water. The aqueous solution is known as lime water and a suspension of slaked lime in water is known as milk of lime."},{"sec": "Calcium hydroxide","ids": [669,670],"t": "Lime water turns milky with CO2: Ca(OH)2 + CO2 → CaCO3 + H2O","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 52,"o": "When carbon dioxide is passed through lime water it turns milky due to the formation of calcium carbonate. Ca(OH)2 + CO2 → CaCO3 + H2O"},{"sec": "Calcium hydroxide","ids": [670,671],"t": "Excess CO2 dissolves it: CaCO3 + CO2 + H2O → Ca(HCO3)2","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 52,"o": "On passing excess of carbon dioxide, the precipitate dissolves to form calcium hydrogencarbonate. CaCO3 + CO2 + H2O → Ca(HCO3)2"},{"sec": "Calcium hydroxide","ids": [671],"t": "Bleaching powder: 2Ca(OH)2 + 2Cl2 → CaCl2 + Ca(OCl)2 + 2H2O","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 52,"o": "Milk of lime reacts with chlorine to form hypochlorite, a constituent of bleaching powder. 2Ca(OH)2 + 2Cl2 → CaCl2 + Ca(OCl)2 + 2H2O"},{"sec": "Calcium hydroxide","ids": [671,672],"t": "Ca(OH)2 uses: mortar, whitewash (disinfectant), glass, tanning, bleaching powder, sugar purification.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 52,"o": "It is used in the preparation of mortar, a building material. It is used in white wash due to its disinfectant nature. It is used in glass making, in tanning industry, for the preparation of bleaching powder and for purification of sugar."},{"sec": "Calcium carbonate","ids": [673],"t": "CaCO3: limestone, chalk, marble; made from slaked lime + CO2 or CaCl2 + Na2CO3.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 52,"o": "Calcium carbonate occurs in nature in several forms like limestone, chalk, marble etc. It can be prepared by passing carbon dioxide through slaked lime or by the addition of sodium carbonate to calcium chloride."},{"sec": "Calcium carbonate","ids": [674],"t": "Ca(OH)2 + CO2 → CaCO3 + H2O; CaCl2 + Na2CO3 → CaCO3 + 2NaCl. Avoid excess CO2 (soluble Ca(HCO3)2 forms).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 52,"o": "Ca(OH)2 + CO2 → CaCO3 + H2O; CaCl2 + Na2CO3 → CaCO3 + 2NaCl. Excess of carbon dioxide should be avoided since this leads to the formation of water soluble calcium hydrogencarbonate."},{"sec": "Calcium carbonate","ids": [675,676,677],"t": "CaCO3: white fluffy powder, almost insoluble; decomposes at 1200 K giving CO2.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 52,"o": "Calcium carbonate is a white fluffy powder. It is almost insoluble in water. When heated to 1200 K, it decomposes to evolve carbon dioxide."},{"sec": "Calcium carbonate","ids": [678,679],"t": "CaCO3 + 2HCl → CaCl2 + H2O + CO2; CaCO3 + H2SO4 → CaSO4 + H2O + CO2","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 52,"o": "It reacts with dilute acid to liberate carbon dioxide. CaCO3 + 2HCl → CaCl2 + H2O + CO2; CaCO3 + H2SO4 → CaSO4 + H2O + CO2"},{"sec": "Calcium carbonate","ids": [679,680],"t": "CaCO3: marble, quick lime; with MgCO3 as flux in iron extraction.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 52,"o": "It is used as a building material in the form of marble and in the manufacture of quick lime. Calcium carbonate along with magnesium carbonate is used as a flux in the extraction of metals such as iron."},{"sec": "Calcium carbonate","ids": [681,682],"t": "Precipitated CaCO3: high-quality paper; antacid, toothpaste abrasive, chewing gum, cosmetic filler.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 52,"o": "Specially precipitated CaCO3 is extensively used in the manufacture of high quality paper. It is also used as an antacid, mild abrasive in tooth paste, a constituent of chewing gum, and a filler in cosmetics."},{"sec": "Plaster of Paris","ids": [683,684],"t": "**Plaster of Paris CaSO4·½H2O**: gypsum CaSO4·2H2O heated to 393 K. 2(CaSO4·2H2O) → 2(CaSO4)·H2O + 3H2O","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 52,"o": "Plaster of Paris, CaSO4·½H2O is a hemihydrate of calcium sulphate. It is obtained when gypsum, CaSO4·2H2O, is heated to 393 K. 2(CaSO4·2H2O) → 2(CaSO4)·H2O + 3H2O"},{"sec": "Plaster of Paris","ids": [684,685],"t": "**Above 393 K: anhydrous CaSO4 = 'dead burnt plaster'.**","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 52,"o": "Above 393 K, no water of crystallisation is left and anhydrous calcium sulphate, CaSO4 is formed. This is known as 'dead burnt plaster'."},{"sec": "Plaster of Paris","ids": [686,687],"t": "Sets with water into a hard solid in 5–15 min.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 53,"o": "It has a remarkable property of setting with water. On mixing with an adequate quantity of water it forms a plastic mass that gets into a hard solid in 5 to 15 minutes."},{"sec": "Plaster of Paris","ids": [688,689,690],"t": "PoP uses: building, plasters, immobilising fractures, dentistry, ornamental casts.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 53,"o": "The largest use of Plaster of Paris is in the building industry as well as plasters. It is used for immoblising the affected part of organ where there is a bone fracture or sprain. It is also employed in dentistry, in ornamental work and for making casts of statues and busts."},{"sec": "Cement","ids": [693],"t": "Cement: lime-rich material (CaO) + clay (SiO2 with Al, Fe, Mg oxides).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 53,"o": "Cement is a product obtained by combining a material rich in lime, CaO with other material such as clay which contains silica, SiO2 along with the oxides of aluminium, iron and magnesium."},{"sec": "Cement","ids": [694],"t": "Portland cement: CaO 50–60%, SiO2 20–25%, Al2O3 5–10%, MgO 2–3%, Fe2O3 1–2%, SO3 1–2%.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 53,"o": "The average composition of Portland cement is: CaO, 50-60%; SiO2, 20-25%; Al2O3, 5-10%; MgO, 2-3%; Fe2O3, 1-2% and SO3, 1-2%."},{"sec": "Cement","ids": [695],"t": "Good cement: SiO2/Al2O3 = 2.5–4; CaO/(SiO2 + Al2O3 + Fe2O3) ≈ 2.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 53,"o": "For a good quality cement, the ratio of silica (SiO2) to alumina (Al2O3) should be between 2.5 and 4 and the ratio of lime (CaO) to the total of the oxides of silicon (SiO2) aluminium (Al2O3) and iron (Fe2O3) should be as close as possible to 2."},{"sec": "Cement","ids": [696,697,698],"t": "Limestone + clay heated → clinker; clinker + 2–3% gypsum → cement.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 53,"o": "The raw materials for the manufacture of cement are limestone and clay. When clay and lime are strongly heated together they fuse and react to form 'cement clinker'. This clinker is mixed with 2-3% by weight of gypsum (CaSO4·2H2O) to form cement."},{"sec": "Cement","ids": [699],"t": "Ingredients: Ca2SiO4 26%, Ca3SiO5 51%, Ca3Al2O6 11%.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 53,"o": "Thus important ingredients present in Portland cement are dicalcium silicate (Ca2SiO4) 26%, tricalcium silicate (Ca3SiO5) 51% and tricalcium aluminate (Ca3Al2O6) 11%."},{"sec": "Cement","ids": [700,701,702],"t": "Setting: hydration and rearrangement; **gypsum only slows setting** so the cement hardens properly.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 53,"o": "When mixed with water, the setting of cement takes place to give a hard mass. This is due to the hydration of the molecules of the constituents and their rearrangement. The purpose of adding gypsum is only to slow down the process of setting of the cement so that it gets sufficiently hardened."}]);
})();
